<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">March on Rome</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">October <a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The March on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> was the mass mobilisation by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-fascist-party">National Fascist Party</a> came to power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in October 1922. While Fascist squads converged on the capital, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-iii">Victor Emmanuel III</a> refused to sign a decree of martial law and on 29 October invited Mussolini to form a government. It marked the beginning of the Fascist regime, which lasted until 1943.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/march-on-rome.jpg" alt="March on Rome" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naples_Fascist_rally_on_24_October_1922_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a> founded the first Italian Fasces of Combat in March 1919, at the start of the Red Biennium, two years of strikes, factory and land occupations and clashes between socialists and landowners. The government tried to act as a neutral mediator and satisfied neither side. Local elites allied with the small Fascist movement, which contained many veterans of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and had a reputation for violence, hoping its paramilitary squads would destroy socialist organisations.</p><p>From 1919 the squadristi, known as Blackshirts, attacked socialist politicians and militants, and in many towns of northern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> they made local government impossible. The police often failed to intervene. The Fascists won 35 seats in the 1921 election, and in November 1921 the movement became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-fascist-party">National Fascist Party</a>, with about 320,000 members. In August 1922 squads razed socialist buildings in Ancona, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 24 October 1922 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> told 60,000 militants at a rally in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>: &quot;Our program is simple: we want to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.&quot; He named four Quadrumvirs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilio-de-bono">Emilio De Bono</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italo-balbo">Italo Balbo</a>, Michele Bianchi and Cesare Maria de Vecchi, to lead the march, while he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. He did not march himself, though he allowed photographs to be taken of him with the marchers.</p><p>As the squads gathered outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, Prime Minister Luigi Facta ordered a state of siege, expecting the King to agree. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-iii">Victor Emmanuel III</a> refused to sign. His reasons have been explained as concern about the army's loyalty and the ambitions of his pro-Fascist cousin, the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aosta">Aosta</a>. The march involved fewer than 30,000 men, but the squadristi already controlled the Po plain and much of the country, and the King feared civil war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> took office within the framework of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statuto-albertino">Statuto Albertino</a>, the Italian constitution, and not by force. Historians therefore describe the March on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> not as the seizure of power that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascism">Fascism</a> later proclaimed, but as the pressure that precipitated a transfer of power, made possible by the surrender of public authorities in the face of intimidation. Many business and financial leaders believed Mussolini could be manipulated.</p><p>He went on to install a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> after the assassination of the socialist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giacomo-matteotti">Giacomo Matteotti</a> on 10 June 1924. At the end of 1923 participants in the march were authorised to wear a Commemorative Medal, and royal decrees between 1926 and 1938 widened eligibility until all Blackshirts and members of the armed forces could wear it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “March on Rome” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>March on Rome</b> in October 1922 the king appointed Benito Mussolini prime minister.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>March on Rome</b> in 1922 brought fascism international attention.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/fascism">Fascism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>The version sung during the <b>March on Rome</b> was arranged by G. Castaldo in 1921 on Blanc's score, with words by Marcello Manni.</blockquote><a href="/songs/giovinezza">Giovinezza</a></li></ul></section>
